A non-jump end of block chains to the next block when no interrupt
is pending, and a control-word change rebuilds the lookup's mode bits.

gen_eob() left for the main loop after every `mov ds/es` in 32-bit
code (HF_ADDSEG may change), every `sti` and `mov ss` (the interrupt
shadow), every `popf`, `iret`, `sysenter`, and every x87 / MXCSR
control-word change (patches 06 and 11 make the mode a TB flag).  The
main loop is needed for one thing only: an interrupt that was pending
while IF was clear has nobody to raise the exit request again once IF
is set, so a block after an IF change cannot be chained blindly.  The
block now loads cpu->interrupt_request and chains through the inline
lookup when it is zero, exiting as before when something waits; an
interrupt that arrives later sets the exit request and stops the next
block at its start, as it always did.  Windows 98's ring-0 entry alone
was five round trips per VxD call (`mov ds`, `mov es`, `sti`, the
shadow's end).

The second half: patch 38 made the lookup's x87 / SSE mode bits the
leaving TB's own constants, which is exactly wrong for a block that
ended because a control word changed -- MSVC's _ftol (fnstcw, fldcw
to truncate, fistp, fldcw back) missed the inline probe twice per call
and took the main loop both times, 220,000 calls a second in 3DMark's
engine.  Such a block rebuilds the bits from env, as patch 20 did.

Measured: main-loop entries from generated code 12.3 million to 5.7
million per ten seconds of the first-person test (8.6 with the chain
alone), first person 19.0 -> 19.1 fps -- the round trips were cheaper
than their count suggested.  Switch: `-accel tcg,eob-chain=off`.
Batteries identical, scripts/test.sh all green.

